 ### Ownership
 
-`lib/instanceAttention.ts` owns the global wire reads (`/api/session`, `/api/sessions/status`, `/api/config/settings` and `/api/fs/home` for the folders a host opens, `/api/git/project-worktrees` for the worktrees outside them, and a session's own detail route for a request that fell outside the page), the ordering and subagent attribution, and the relay transport. `lib/attentionDirectories.ts` owns the folder rule both halves apply. `hooks/useInstanceAttentionPolling.ts` owns the desktop poller and its reachability decisions; `hooks/useLocalInstanceAttention.ts` publishes this window's own instance from the live stores. `stores/useInstanceAttentionStore.ts` holds the one sorted list every surface reads. `lib/instanceAttentionJump.ts` owns the shared switch-and-open path, and `lib/desktopHostActions.ts` owns the saved-host switch and the return to the app's own server.
+`lib/instanceAttention.ts` owns the global wire reads (`/api/session`, `/api/sessions/status`, `/api/config/settings` and `/api/fs/home` for the folders a host opens, `/api/git/project-worktrees` for the worktrees outside them, and a session's own detail route for a request that fell outside the page), the projection both halves share (`instanceSessionSummary`), the ordering and row attribution, and the relay transport. Which sessions an instance lists is the sidebar's own row rule (`components/session/sidebar/list/sidebarRows.ts`), fed this window's stores by the local half and a host's own answers by the poller. `hooks/useInstanceAttentionPolling.ts` owns the desktop poller and its reachability decisions; `hooks/useLocalInstanceAttention.ts` publishes this window's own instance from the live stores. `stores/useInstanceAttentionStore.ts` holds the one sorted list every surface reads. `lib/instanceAttentionJump.ts` owns the shared switch-and-open path, and `lib/desktopHostActions.ts` owns the saved-host switch and the return to the app's own server.
 
 ### Invariants
 
-- Both halves apply one folder rule, so the drawer cannot show a session the instance on screen hides. The local half filters with `isSessionAdmitted`; a polled half filters with the folders that host reports. A host that cannot answer (an unreadable settings or home route) leaves its rows standing rather than reading as "no folders", while a host that answers without any project is a real answer and admits only chats roots and spaces.
+- A session is an item for an instance exactly when that instance's sidebar gives it a row or nests it inside one, and it reports through that row: a subagent, an operator child (linked by metadata, with no `parentID`), a review or a /btw session lights its root's row, and a session with no row is never listed. Both halves build the rows with `buildSidebarRowBase` and `resolveSessionRowOf` over every zone, so the list for an instance is the same whether the window shows it or polls it. A /btw session, which the sidebar keeps out of its tree and shows inside its original, takes the original's row.
+- A failed or partial read never hides a waiting session. The row map says, per session, which row it sits in or that it has none (`null`); a session the map does not name is unknown and is shown on its own, under its loaded top-level parent, as before rows existed. Rows travel with the sessions they judged: a poll whose session list answered but whose settings or home read failed carries no rows, so every session is unknown, instead of judging newer sessions by older rows. A pending request whose session is in no list (its detail read failed, or it never had one) is an untitled item. The local half publishes nothing until this window's global session list has loaded, and passes the sessions that can make an item (active ones, live-gap ones, and those with a pending request that the global list or a directory store holds), so its untitled fallback covers only a request for a session no store of this window holds; without the load check, every request for a hidden session became an untitled row that opened into a trust prompt. A pending session whose parent chain (`parentID`, or the metadata parent link `projectSession` folds into it) cannot be read in full is left unknown too, so it shows on its own rather than vanishing with a parent the sidebar could not place. A host that answers without any project is a real answer and lists only chats and pinned sessions.
+- A host's session records go through `projectSession`, the projection this window's own client uses, so parent links from metadata, review and /btw origins, pins and unread marks read the same on both halves. The reader requires only what the row rule cannot do without (an id, a folder, an update time) and fills the counters it does not read; a record without those is left out, and a page in which no record could be read is a failed read, not an empty host. Metadata that is not a JSON object is dropped; it cannot carry a parent link, which only lives in an object.
+- A failed turn is `failed` only while `isSessionUnreadWithError` holds, as in the tray: a session marked unread after its failed turn was seen is plain unread.
+- This window's own publish leaves the store untouched when it changes no item, so a streamed turn does not re-render the surfaces. Each publish still rebuilds the sidebar tree when the global list changed (the builder is cached by input identity and shared with the tray); at 300 sessions, 100 streamed updates cost 100 builds and about 60 ms in all, and 3,000 archived sessions beside them add about 70 ms, so it is not debounced.
 - `lib/pendingFormVerification.ts` decides which indexed forms the server's own form list no longer holds: the host's cross-directory index keeps a form whose settlement event it missed (a question tool call that ended `error` never sent one), so the drawer asks OpenCode again for the directories of the forms it would show. A directory that could not be read keeps its forms, since absence from an unreadable answer proves nothing. The local half applies the plan and settles the index; a polled host's rows are filtered from the same answer inside the reader.
 - A pending form is checked against OpenCode's own form list before it becomes a row: the host's cross-directory index keeps a form whose settlement event it missed (a question tool call that ended `error` never sent one), so a polled host's rows are filtered by `/api/form` for that host, and the local half settles the index's stale forms from the same read when it mounts or the drawer is refreshed. A failed read keeps the form, since absence from an unreadable answer proves nothing.
